                <pb facs="0137" n="65" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg65"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 14716/2–3.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to Minister Rockhill.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1908-10-27"><hi rendition="#italic">October 27,
                        1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 507.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the correspondence
                    between the consulate general at Hankow and the legation, in regard to the Tai
                    Hong Co., and the legation’s No. 1636, of June 22, 1908, in this connection to
                    the consulate general at Hankow, there is inclosed herewith a copy of the
                    department’s instruction, of to-day’s date, to the consulate general at
                    Hankow.</p>
                <p>It is believed that the substance of this instruction may be of interest to
                    certain of the various consuls in China, and you may, therefore, wish to send
                    copies thereof to those offices where, in your opinion, the correspondence may
                    be of value for future reference and guidance.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d82-a1.xml" xpointer="d82-a1"/>
            </div>
